<DocsLayout
  title="Getting Started — Quakboard Docs"
  heading="Getting Started"
  summary="Install Quakboard, grant the one permission it needs, and make your first paste from history in under two minutes."
>
  <h2>Install</h2>
  <p>
    Download the build for your platform from the <a href="/download"
      >download page</a
    >, then open it like any other app:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>macOS</strong> — open the <code>.dmg</code> and drag Quakboard into Applications.</li>
    <li><strong>Windows</strong> — run the <code>.msi</code> and follow the installer.</li>
    <li><strong>Linux</strong> — mark the <code>.AppImage</code> executable, or install the <code>.deb</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The app launches into the background. On macOS it lives in the menu bar
    rather than the Dock, so don't worry if no window appears at first — that's
    expected.
  </p>

  <h2>Grant the paste permission</h2>
  <p>
    To paste an item back into another app, Quakboard needs one OS-level
    permission. You're prompted the first time you try to paste:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>macOS</strong> — enable Quakboard under <code
        >System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → Accessibility</code
      >.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Windows / Linux</strong> — no extra permission needed; pasting
      works immediately.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Quakboard never reads other apps' contents — the permission is only used to
    send a paste keystroke into the app you were last using.
  </p>

  <h2>Your first paste from history</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Copy a few different things — a URL, a paragraph, a snippet of code.</li>
    <li>
      Press the global shortcut <code>⌘⇧V</code> (macOS) or <code
        >Ctrl+Shift+V</code
      > (Windows/Linux). The history window appears at your cursor.
    </li>
    <li>Start typing to filter, use <code>↑ / ↓</code> to move the selection.</li>
    <li>Press <code>Enter</code> to paste the highlighted item where you were.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    That's the whole loop. Everything else — pinning, search operators, sync —
    builds on top of it.
  </p>

  <h2>Pin something you reuse</h2>
  <p>
    Hover any history item and click the pin, or select it and press <code
      >⌘P</code
    >. Pinned items become <strong>snippets</strong>: they don't age out of
    history, you can give them a name, and they float to the top of the list.
    Good candidates are email signatures, shipping addresses, and commands you
    run often.
  </p>

  <h2>Next steps</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Learn the full <a href="/docs/keyboard-shortcuts">keyboard shortcuts</a> —
      Quakboard is faster without the mouse.
    </li>
    <li>
      Turn on <a href="/docs/sync">Sync</a> if you use more than one machine.
    </li>
    <li>
      Read exactly <a href="/docs/privacy">what's stored and where</a> before you
      trust it with sensitive clips.
    </li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
